                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="592-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="592-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>R&#00216;DE NT55</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Detachable-Capsule Pencil Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The NT5 is a small-diaphragm pencil condenser mic that ships with two interchangeable capsule heads, providing either a cardioid or omni response. The mic body contains switches giving access to a 3-way pad (0, -10, -20dB) and 3-way high-pass filter (flat, 75Hz, 150Hz).</p><p>The NT45 capsules are compatible with the NT4, NT5, NT55, and NT6 mics. Their diaphragms measure 1/2&#39;&#39; in diameter and are externally-biased (&#8220;true&#8221; condenser) designs.</p><p>The mic includes a basic clip (p/n RM5), the NT45-O Omni capsule head, the NT45-C Cardioid capsule head, a metal storage box, and a 10-year warranty.</p><p>Also available as a matched pair (p/n NT55MP).</p><blockquote><p><cite>Electronic Musician</cite></p><p>The highpass filter employs a gentle 12 dB-per-octave curve, which is a good thing, because the NT55 doesn&#8217;t have an overblown bottom end to start with. I wouldn&#8217;t call it thin, but it would not be my first choice for low-end instruments such as bass or a surdo drum.</p><p>The beauty of the NT55 is in its midrange detail, which I really loved. The sound of my fingerpicking was very clear, yet strumming with a pick did not result in a &#8220;splanky&#8221; sound, which can often happen with small-capsule condensers on acoustic guitar.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Rode/NT55">R&#00216;DE NT55</a> is also known as: NT55MP.</p>
